WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.050
I now introduce Gibbs sampling, another 
randomized algorithm for motif finding. 

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:16.200
Gibbs sampling works somewhat
similarly to the randomize motif search.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.510
The biggest difference is that randomized

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.500
motif search is a rather reckless
algorithm.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.790
It can actually change all found motifs
that

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:30.530
at every iteration depending on what are the 
profile most probable k-mers.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:31.030
It allows to move 

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:35.390
very quickly in
the space of the solutions

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:39.600
but there is a danger that you already
came close to the correct motif, 

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.240
and then jump away from this motif, because
randomized motif search

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:47.560
moves so rapidly through the space of all
possible motif.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.860
Gibbs Sampler is more cautious.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:56.140
It actually changes only one
of the chosen motifs at every step. 

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.990
This slide shows the key difference between

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:01.870
Randomized Motif Search and Gibbs
Sampling.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:05.110
Instead of showing you the pseudocode for
Gibbs Sampler,

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:09.970
let me show you first how it works.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:15.490
Let's first randomly select motifs shown

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:21.140
in bold in this slide.
In the set of Dna strings, as before,

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:24.790
blue elements shows implanted motifs, and
bold

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:27.940
elements showed motifs that
we randomly selected.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.550
After we've done it, let's do something
strange.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:34.890
Let's actually remove one of the found
motifs

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.740
along with the sequence it belongs to.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.730
In our example, we remove the third motif,

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:44.440
and remove the third Dna sequence from
consideration.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.330
We'll consider

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:54.220
the resulting set of just four motifs, and
build the Count matrix based on this set.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:59.600
And then afterwards, construct Profile
matrix based on this Count matrix.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:04.118
And after we constructed the Profile
matrix, we will calculate the probability

00:02:04.118 --> 00:02:11.874
of all k-mers in the deleted string.
Afterwards, we will do the following,

00:02:11.874 --> 00:02:18.590
again relying heavily on rolling the die.
There are seven probabilites we computed

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:24.610
and we will roll a seven-sided die
according to the probabilities we computed.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:26.900
It will be heavily loaded die

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:33.200
with probabilities of sides proportional to
the probabilities that we computed.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.070
After we toss this die, we choose a new
starting

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:41.530
position of the Motif and proceed in this
way.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:45.710
This is new a substituted position in the
deleted sequence.

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:52.010
So that's how it works and then of course,
it iterates until motifs keep improving, 

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:56.950
or until a certain number of
iterations (let's say 100,000 iterations).

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:02.405
So, here is the outline of Gibbs Sampler. We
randomly select a

00:03:02.405 --> 00:03:05.220
k-mer in each sequence, as before.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:09.080
We randomly choose one of the selected k-mers
from sequence

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:13.690
called Sequence* and remove it from the set
of motifs.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:16.850
We create profile from the remaining
motifs.

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:22.000
For each k-mer at the position i in
Sequence, we compute its

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.570
probability of being generated by profile,

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:30.330
resulting in n-k+1 probabilities. 

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:35.722
Afterwards. we generate a die with 
n-k+1 sides

00:03:35.722 --> 00:03:42.320
whose probabilities of sides are
proportional to probabilities we computed.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:47.640
We roll this die and,
depending on the outcome

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:52.620
of the die, we turn back this motif

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:59.186
number i to the sequence that we removed.  And
we reiterate.

00:03:59.186 --> 00:04:04.460
So let's once again look at how Gibbs
Sampler work.

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:07.580
Let's assume that we've done the first
stages and we're

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:13.080
about to generate the, probabilities of
every k-mer in the sequence.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.910
I am still considering the same example I
considered before.

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:18.470
We generated

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:22.360
our seven probabilities, but now let's pay
attention of what these

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:27.180
probabilities are. And it turned out that

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:31.120
six out of seven probabilities.
I actually zeroes.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:38.290
Should we roll a one-sided die?

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:43.755
Four centuries ago, Oliver Cromwell warned us
against rolling

00:04:43.755 --> 00:04:45.810
one-sided dice.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:49.169
He told in his famous appeal to the court
of Scotland.

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:55.960
trying to convince them that their
alliance with the king was an error.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:59.460
He said: "I beseech you, in the bowels of

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:04.030
Christ, think it possible that you may be
mistaken."

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:09.390
Cromwell Rule in statistics implies that the
use of probabilities

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:12.880
0 and 1 should be avoided,

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.665
unless we talk about purely logical
statements.

00:05:17.665 --> 00:05:19.970
Thus, we should actually give a

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:24.040
little probability even to extremely
unlikely event.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:27.330
For example, we should give small
probability to

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:30.910
the fact that the sun won't rise,
tomorrow.

